\DOC WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_NOSTACK_TAC

\TYPE {WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_NOSTACK_TAC : thm -> thm -> thm -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a Windows-ABI {ensures} specification goal for an x86 subroutine by
deriving it from a standard-ABI core correctness theorem for the same code.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are {winmc : thm}, the definition of the Windows-ABI machine-code
byte list for the routine; {stdmc : thm}, the definition of the corresponding
standard-ABI machine-code byte list; and {coreth : thm}, the already-proved
specification of the standard-ABI core, given either as a plain universally
quantified {ensures} statement or as one wrapped in an existential over an
event function. It applies to an assumption-free, universally quantified
Windows-ABI {ensures} goal, possibly preceded by an existential quantifier over
an event-trace function, and usually closes it outright:
{
   ?- t
   ========================  WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_NOSTACK_TAC winmc stdmc coreth
   (no subgoals)
}
\noindent In the existentially quantified event-trace form it can instead leave
a single residual goal, whose conclusion {u} is the existential event-trace
obligation about the wrapper's trace and whose new assumption list {A}
describes the symbolic state: the instantiated non-overlapping side conditions,
the core theorem's event and memory-access facts, the loaded code and
register/stack contents at the final state, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation, and the final program counter, stack pointer and event trace:
{
   ?- t
   ========================  WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_NOSTACK_TAC winmc stdmc coreth
   A ?- u
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, on goals of this one shape
with no initial assumptions.

\ENDDOC
